\label{asm:anonemp}
The target set \(\mathcal{A}\) is nonempty and closed, and there exists a nonnegative measurable function \(V:S\to\mathbb R_{\ge0}\) satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{eq:comparison}
\alpha_1(d_\mathcal A(s))\le V(s)\le\alpha_2(d_\mathcal A(s))
\end{equation}
on the reachable domain, where \(\alpha_1,\alpha_2\) are continuous, strictly increasing, unbounded functions that vanish at zero (class \(\mathcal K_\infty\)). In particular, \(V(s)=0\) exactly when \(s\in\mathcal A\).

\label{thm:exact}
Assume well-defined evolution, the correct posterior kernel, \eqref{eq:comparison}, \cref{asm:feasible}, finite \(B_0\), and \(\mathbb E V(s_0)<\infty\). For every integer \(N\ge1\),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound}
c\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\mathbb E V(s_k)\le\mathbb E V(s_0)+B_0.
\end{equation}
Consequently \(V(s_k)\to0\) almost surely and in mean and \(d_\mathcal A(s_k)\to0\) almost surely. For \(r>0\) and \(T_r=\inf\{k\ge0:d_\mathcal A(s_k)\le r\}\),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:entry}
\mathbb E T_r\le\frac{\mathbb E V(s_0)+B_0}{c\alpha_1(r)}.
\end{equation}

With \(W_k=V(s_k)+B_k\ge0\), \eqref{eq:drift} and the update of \(B_k\) give
\begin{equation}\label{eq:supermart}
\mathbb E[W_{k+1}\mid\mathcal F_k]\le W_k-cV(s_k).
\end{equation}
Taking expectations and summing over \(k=0,\ldots,N-1\) gives \eqref{eq:bound} because \(W_N\ge0\). Monotone convergence gives \(\mathbb E\sum_kV(s_k)\le(\mathbb E V(s_0)+B_0)/c\). This nonnegative series is finite almost surely, so \(V(s_k)\to0\) almost surely; summability of its expectations also implies convergence in mean. The lower comparison in \eqref{eq:comparison} gives convergence in distance. For each \(k<T_r\), \(V(s_k)\ge\alpha_1(r)\). Sum these inequalities and take expectations to obtain \eqref{eq:entry}.

\label{prop:sample}
Under the physical and comparison conditions of \cref{thm:exact}, the sampling conditions above, and an accepted action returned after finitely many samples at every decision, suppose \(\sum_{k\ge0}\delta_k\le\Delta<\infty\) almost surely. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:samplebound}
c\sum_{k\ge0}\mathbb E V(s_k)\le\mathbb E V(s_0)+B_0+V_{\max}\Delta.
\end{equation}
The executed states satisfy \(d_\mathcal A(s_k)\to0\) almost surely, and \eqref{eq:entry} holds with \(V_{\max}\Delta\) added to its numerator.

Let \(\mathcal G_k\) extend \(\mathcal F_k\) by the full computational random stream. The selected action and sample count are \(\mathcal G_k\)-measurable, and conditional independence gives \(\mathbb E[V(s_{k+1})\mid\mathcal G_k]=\mu_k(a_k)\). On the coverage event in \eqref{eq:coverage}, the slack update gives \(\mathbb E[W_{k+1}\mid\mathcal G_k]\le W_k-cV(s_k)\); on its complement, \(W_{k+1}\le V_{\max}+B_k\). Thus
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sample-drift}
\mathbb E[W_{k+1}\mid\mathcal F_k]\le W_k-cV(s_k)+V_{\max}\delta_k.
\end{equation}
Summation and the argument of \cref{thm:exact} give the claims.
